    <h2>GMT vs AKDT</h2>
    <p>
      Understanding the distinction between Greenwich Mean Time and Alaska Daylight Time requires separating astronomical designations from seasonal standards. GMT represents a time standard historically determined by the Royal Observatory in Greenwich. It does not adjust for daylight saving, making it a constant timezone baseline.
    </p>
    <p>
      AKDT is a specific offset (UTC-8) observed by Alaska during summer. When people search for <strong>gmt to akdt converter</strong>, they are referring to Alaska Time (AT) in general, which changes seasonally. It is critical to differentiate between the standard winter offset (AKST) and the daylight summer offset (AKDT) to avoid scheduling discrepancies.
    </p>
