    <h2>AKDT vs AKST</h2>
    <p>
      The core of Alaska's timekeeping lies in the transition between Alaska Daylight Time (AKDT) and Alaska Standard Time (AKST). AKST operates at a UTC-9 offset, meaning it is nine hours behind Greenwich. During standard winter time (early November to mid-March), this offset is active across the state.
    </p>
    <p>
      In contrast, AKDT operates at a UTC-8 offset, which is eight hours behind Greenwich. The state transitions to AKDT on the second Sunday of March to extend evening daylight hours. This seasonal change shifts the timezone gap relative to Greenwich, changing from 9 hours in winter to 8 hours in summer.
    </p>
  </div>
  
