    <h2>Does Alaska Always Use AKDT?</h2>
    <p>
      No, Alaska does not always use AKDT. The state observes Alaska Daylight Time only during the Daylight Saving Time window, which runs from mid-March to early November. During the winter, the state transitions back to Alaska Standard Time (AKST), which operates at UTC-9.
    </p>
    <p>
      Additionally, certain parts of Alaska, such as the Aleutian Islands west of 169°30'W, follow Hawaii-Aleutian Time. These islands observe Hawaii-Aleutian Daylight Time (HDT, UTC-9) in summer and Hawaii-Aleutian Standard Time (HST, UTC-10) in winter.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

